.TH GREW 1 "2026" "grew 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
grew \- track what is eating your disk, over time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grew
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B du
and
.B ncdu
show which directories are large right now. Neither shows which directories
.I grew.
.PP
.B grew
records directory sizes periodically and diffs those snapshots, so you can ask
what consumed 40GB since last week and get the specific directory responsible.
.PP
Because directory sizes are cumulative, a single large subdirectory inflates every
parent above it.
.B grew
suppresses any directory whose change is explained by one child, reporting only the
directory actually to blame. Use
.B \-\-all
to see the full chain.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B snap " [" \fIpath\fR "]"
Record directory sizes beneath \fIpath\fR (default: current directory). A directory
inside the tree that cannot be listed is counted as unreadable and the snapshot is
still taken. A \fIpath\fR containing \fB..\fR is resolved the way the kernel reads it,
so \fBlnk/..\fR (with \fBlnk\fR a symlink) is the real parent, as for \fBdu\fR.
.TP
.B growth " [" \fIpath\fR "]"
Show what changed between two snapshots. Defaults to the two most recent.
.TP
.B top " [" \fIpath\fR "]"
List the largest directories in the most recent snapshot.
.TP
.B list " [" \fIpath\fR "]"
List recorded snapshots with their totals and change since the previous one.
.TP
.B prune " [" \fIpath\fR "]"
Delete old snapshots, keeping the newest \fB\-\-keep\fR (default 20) of
.I each
directory, so a busy directory cannot push a quieter one's history out. With a
\fIpath\fR, only that directory's snapshots are touched.
.PP
\fBlist\fR, \fBtop\fR, \fBgrowth\fR and \fBprune\fR given a \fIpath\fR with no
snapshots name that path and say how many other directories do have snapshots.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-db " FILE"
Database location. Defaults to \fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/grew/snapshots.db\fR. The name
\fB:memory:\fR is refused, because sqlite would keep the snapshot in memory and
lose it when \fBgrew\fR exits; write \fB./:memory:\fR for a file of that name.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output. Supported by every command, including \fBprune\fR.
Names are JSON-escaped; in the plain output, control characters in a directory name
(escape, newline, bell and the like) are written as \fB\\x1b\fR, \fB\\x0a\fR and so on, so
that a name cannot drive the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour. Colour is also disabled when output is not a terminal or when
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.PP
\fB\-\-db\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR and \fB\-\-no\-color\fR are accepted either before or after the
command: \fBgrew \-\-json list\fR and \fBgrew list \-\-json\fR are the same.
.SS snap options
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-size " SIZE"
Ignore directories smaller than \fISIZE\fR (default 1M). Keeps snapshots small.
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " N"
Only record directories at most \fIN\fR levels deep.
.TP
.B \-\-apparent
Use apparent file size rather than allocated blocks. As with
\fBdu \-\-apparent\-size\fR, files and symlinks are counted and directories are not.
.TP
.B \-\-cross\-filesystem
Descend into other mounted filesystems. Off by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " PATH"
Skip \fIPATH\fR. May be given more than once. A relative \fIPATH\fR is taken from the
current directory, as for any other argument, not from the snapshot root. One that is not
inside the snapshot, or does not exist, excludes nothing and is reported on standard
error (also with \fB\-q\fR, so that a cron job with a wrong path is noticed); the
snapshot is still taken.
.TP
.B \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output.
.SS growth options
.TP
.BI \-\-since " DURATION"
Compare against the newest snapshot at least \fIDURATION\fR old, e.g. \fB7d\fR,
\fB12h\fR, \fB2w\fR. A number and a unit (\fBs m h d w\fR) are required, and the
duration must be greater than zero. If the history does not go back that far, the
oldest snapshot is used and a note is printed on standard error (never on standard
output, so \fB\-\-json\fR stays clean).
.TP
.BI \-\-from " ID" ", " \-\-to " ID"
Compare two specific snapshots by id, as shown by \fBgrew list\fR. The two must be
different snapshots of the same directory.
.TP
.BI \-n " N" ", " \-\-top " N"
Limit output to \fIN\fR entries (default 20).
.TP
.B \-\-all
Show parent directories instead of only the responsible one.
.TP
.B \-\-shrink
Include directories that got smaller. Without it, a tree that only shrank reports that
nothing grew and how many directories shrank, rather than that nothing changed.
Changes of equal size are listed in path order, so the output (and what \fB\-n\fR cuts)
is the same on every run.
.SS prune options
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " N"
Keep the newest \fIN\fR snapshots of each directory (default 20). \fB\-\-keep 0\fR
removes every snapshot.
.TP
.B \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing on success, for cron. \fB\-\-json\fR still prints its result.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Take a snapshot now:
.B grew snap /home/me
.TP
See what changed in the last week:
.B grew growth --since 7d
.TP
Snapshot daily from cron, keeping two months:
.nf
.B 0 3 * * * grew snap /home -q && grew prune --keep 60 -q
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/grew/snapshots.db
Snapshot database. Falls back to \fI~/.local/share/grew\fR. It lists the large
directories of the disk, so \fBgrew\fR creates the file with mode 0600 and a directory
it has to create with 0700 (both less the umask); existing ones are left as they are.
Only \fBsnap\fR creates
it; the other commands report that nothing is recorded yet and leave no file behind.
\fBlist\fR, \fBtop\fR and \fBgrowth\fR also work on a read-only database, and
create nothing, not even \fI\-wal\fR or \fI\-shm\fR files. \fBsnap\fR and
\fBprune\fR on a database file that is read-only are refused before it is opened,
so a failed write leaves nothing behind either; the message names the file to make
writable. An empty file counts as a
database with nothing in it. A SQLite file that grew did not create is refused and
never written to; having a table called \fBmeta\fR is not enough to count as grew's, the
table must have grew's columns and a \fBschema\fR row and nothing foreign may sit beside it.
A \fBlist\fR, \fBtop\fR or \fBgrowth\fR on a database that does not exist, or is an empty
file, names that file in its message, so a mistyped \fB\-\-db\fR is easy to spot. Paths are
stored as bytes (BLOB columns); databases from grew 1.0 stored them as TEXT and are
still read correctly. The schema version is unchanged (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. A directory inside the scanned tree that cannot be read does not change
this: it is counted (\fBsnap\fR prints how many were unreadable) and the scan goes
on. This includes \fBlist\fR and \fBprune\fR finding nothing for a path: an
empty answer is still an answer, and a nightly \fBprune\fR should not fail on it. It
also includes \fBsnap\fR when the snapshot was saved but its summary could not be
written (a full or closed standard output): a note on standard error says so.
.TP
.B 1
Error, with a message saying what to do. This includes \fBtop\fR and \fBgrowth\fR
having no snapshots to work from (fewer than two for \fBgrowth\fR), a database that
cannot be used (including \fBsnap\fR or \fBprune\fR on a read-only file), and a
\fBsnap\fR path that does not exist, is not a directory, or lies below a directory
that cannot be searched.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, such as an unknown option or \fB\-\-since 0s\fR.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (Ctrl-C), also when pressed twice. Nothing is left half-written.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
When the database cannot be used, the message says why and what to do. A locked
database means another \fBgrew\fR is writing: try again. A read-only file or
directory means fixing the permissions or using \fB\-\-db\fR; a filesystem mounted
read-only is named as such, because changing permissions does not help there. A full disk means
freeing space. Only a file that looks corrupt is told to be moved aside; \fBgrew\fR
never advises deleting a database that may be healthy. A SQLite file belonging to
another program, a path that is not a regular file (such as \fI/dev/null\fR), and a
database holding the wrong kind of data are each reported as what they are. On a
terminal that cannot show the arrow and ellipsis characters, \fB\->\fR and
\fB...\fR are used instead.
.SH NOTES
Sizes count allocated blocks, matching
.BR du (1),
or with \fB\-\-apparent\fR file and symlink sizes, matching \fBdu \-\-apparent\-size\fR.
Hardlinked files, and hardlinked symlinks, are counted once. Symlinks are never
followed.
.PP
The totals agree with \fBdu \-x\fR with one exception, by design: \fBsnap\fR does not
descend into \fI/proc\fR, \fI/sys\fR, \fI/dev\fR, \fI/run\fR or \fI/tmp/.X11\-unix\fR
below its root, even where one of them is on the same filesystem (as in many containers),
because they report meaningless sizes or never end. Snapping one of them directly, as the
root, does scan it.
.PP
Snapshots taken with \fB\-\-apparent\fR by grew 1.0 counted each directory's own
size, which \fBdu \-\-apparent\-size\fR does not. Nothing in the database tells them
from newer ones, so \fBgrowth\fR across the upgrade shows a drop equal to the sum of
the directory sizes. Take a fresh \fB\-\-apparent\fR snapshot and compare from there,
or \fBprune \-\-keep 0\fR the old ones.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR du (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR ncdu (1)
